 "standard_text": "Solve simultaneous linear equations; solve one linear and one quadratic equation together; solve word problems. (summary of the NERDC SSS 2 Mathematics curriculum)",
 "meta_desc": "Free SSS 2 maths worksheets on simultaneous equations: elimination, substitution, one linear and one quadratic equation, and word problems. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Make y the subject, then substitute",
 "key_sub": "Each x gives its own y.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>Two linear equations: add or subtract them to remove one letter.</li><li>One linear and one quadratic: make one letter the subject of the linear equation and put it into the quadratic.</li><li>Solve the quadratic and find the matching value of the other letter for each root.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>y = x + 1 and y = x² − 1</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>x² − x − 2 = 0</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>x = 2 or −1</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: (2, 3) and (−1, 0)</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pairing the x and y values wrongly</td><td>Work out y from each x.</td></tr><tr><td>Substituting into the quadratic</td><td>Use the linear equation to find y.</td></tr><tr><td>Stopping at one root</td><td>A line can meet a curve twice.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
